<Project Sdk="Dalamud.NET.Sdk/15.0.0">

  <!--
    BozjaBuddyReborn - a Bozja/Zadnor field-operation orchestrator.

    Build requirements (same pin as the rest of this machine's plugins):
      .NET 10 SDK              (Dalamud API 15 targets net10.0-windows)
      Dalamud dev libraries at %AppData%\XIVLauncher\addon\Hooks\dev
                               (or set DALAMUD_HOME)
      Verified against Dalamud 15.0.2.3 / FFXIVClientStructs 7.51.0.8543 /
      Lumina.Excel 7.5.1.0.

    Change the SDK version if your installed Dalamud API level differs, or the
    FFXIVClientStructs members this plugin reads (PublicContentBozja, BozjaState,
    DynamicEventContainer, TerritoryInfo) may not line up.

    BUILD DISCIPLINE (mirrors Relicable):
      Every change is compile-verified in Debug AND Release before being handed back,
      and the version below is bumped by one on the third component each time so the
      dev plugin actually reloads. The changelog under <Version> records what each
      build changed and why, newest first.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>

    <!-- Bumped by 0.0.1.0 each change so the dev plugin shows the update. This build
         (CRASH FIX - the sign-up button press passed a null AtkEventData and the recruitment
         addon dereferences it):
         1.0.27.0 could hard-crash the game client from the sign-up path. Reported sequence:
         signed up for Castrum Lacus Litore through the plugin, withdrew by hand, then signed up
         for an ordinary Critical Engagement. Crash dump crash-20260827005143 names it exactly:

           Unhandled native exception, code C0000005 (access violation), reading address 6
           [0] Client::UI::AddonMYCBattleAreaInfo.ReceiveEvent+0x5E
           [4] ECommons...ClickHelperExtensions.ClickAddonButton(AtkComponentButton, AtkUnitBase*)
           [5] BozjaBuddyReborn.Automation.SignUpRunner.Click
           [6] BozjaBuddyReborn.Automation.SignUpRunner.StepRegister

         THE CAUSE. AtkUnitBase.ReceiveEvent takes FOUR parameters, the last being AtkEventData*.
         ECommons' convenience overload - the one 1.0.26.0 adopted - is a single line that leaves
         that parameter null:

             addon->ReceiveEvent(evt->State.EventType, (int)evt->Param, btnRes.AtkEventManager.Event);

         Most addons never read it, which is why that overload is used safely all over ECommons.
         AddonMYCBattleAreaInfo does read it: null plus a small field offset is an access at
         address 6, which is precisely what the dump records. Registering had worked in earlier
         sessions because the branch taken inside ReceiveEvent depends on the event type and
         param - switching between engagements took a branch that touches the event data.

         THE FIX. The press now goes through the full component path, which is what an actual
         mouse click does: a constructed AtkEvent naming the button as target and the addon as
         listener, plus a real (empty) input-data buffer rather than a null pointer. The event
         type and param are taken from the button's own attached event, preferring an explicit
         MouseClick/ButtonClick if the chain carries one, with a bounded walk so a circular chain
         cannot hang the framework thread.

         AND EVERY POINTER ON THAT PATH IS NOW CHECKED BEFORE USE, because an access violation is
         a process kill rather than a catchable exception - a try/catch around this would have
         caught nothing, so the guarding has to be preventive. Checked: the agent module and the
         addon manager singletons, the button's owner node, and the attached event itself. A
         button with no attached event is no longer clicked at all - that is the state a row is in
         while the list rebuilds, which is exactly what the reported sequence produces - and a
         refused press no longer advances the phase, so the flow retries instead of waiting on a
         press that never happened.

         Also hardened: the node walk skips any UldManager that has not finished loading or has a
         null node list, and nothing is clicked until the window has been loaded and visible for
         600ms, so a list still being populated is never touched.

         ONE THING THIS CONFIRMS, from the dump rather than by inference: the runtime addon name
         really is MYCBattleAreaInfo. That had been flagged as unverified since it was only ever
         attested as a shipped ULD filename.
         Compile-verified Debug and Release (0 warnings), from a clean obj/bin.

         Prior build - 1.0.27.0
         (Commence now runs - 1.0.26.0 cancelled its own attempt one tick after registering):
         Reported: 1.0.26.0 fixed signing up, but it still never commences. Two defects, both
         introduced by 1.0.26.0's own rewrite, either of which alone stops the second phase dead.

         1. THE ATTEMPT CANCELLED ITSELF THE MOMENT REGISTRATION LANDED. 1.0.26.0 added a
         precondition reading "already in an engagement, nothing to sign up for", backed by
         DynamicEventContainer.CurrentEventIndex >= 0. That was the wrong reading of the
         container: CurrentEventIndex goes non-negative when you REGISTER, not when you are
         deployed - the container starts naming the engagement as soon as you are associated with
         it. So the tick after a successful Register press, the guard fired and ended the attempt
         with "Already in engagement #N", before the lottery had even run and long before a
         Commence button could exist. Registering therefore worked and commencing was
         unreachable, exactly as reported. The container is still the right SUCCESS signal; it is
         simply not a precondition once the flow is underway, so it is now checked once, when the
         attempt starts, and after that only as the outcome of the Commence press.

         2. THE WINDOW COULD NOT BE REOPENED FOR THE SECOND PHASE. The "ask the agent to show the
         window" latch was per ATTEMPT rather than per PHASE, so once it had been set the runner
         would never ask again. Pressing Register frequently closes the recruitment window (and
         the player may close it), and the Commence press has to happen at that same window a
         minute or more later - so the second phase sat waiting for a window it had decided it had
         already asked for, then timed out. The latch is now cleared on every phase change, and a
         window that closes while awaiting the draw is reopened (capped at 8 reopens, with an
         overall 5-minute ceiling on the attempt so a window that genuinely cannot open still
         ends it).

         3. SUCCESS WAS BEING INFERRED FROM THE SAME BAD SIGNAL. Having pressed Commence,
         1.0.26.0 declared success as soon as RegisteredEventId was set - which, per (1), was
         already true before the press. It would have reported success for a Commence that did
         nothing. Success is now observed directly: the Commence button going away, or the
         engagement actually being under way.

         4. A DOUBLE-CLICK COULD HAVE WITHDRAWN THE REGISTRATION. It is ONE button whose label
         changes - Register, then Withdraw, then Commence - and the label lags the press by a
         frame or two. A second click fired into that gap lands on the same button now meaning
         Withdraw and cancels the registration just made. Added a 1.5s interlock after every
         click, and the "registration lapsed, try again" transition now requires the evidence to
         persist for 3s rather than acting on one frame of a stale label.

         5. THE CONFIRMATION HANDLER WAS MISSING RATHER THAN WRONG. 1.0.26.0 removed the old
         indiscriminate Yes-clicker (correctly - it answered any prompt on screen, unread, on the
         first tick, and reported success for it) but did not put anything back, so a Yes/No
         raised by a Commence press would have gone unanswered. There is now a narrow one: only
         after one of our own clicks has actually gone out, only for a prompt whose text mentions
         this flow, and never through a disabled button. Unrelated prompts are logged and left
         alone.

         DIAGNOSTICS, because the remaining unknown is a string. The second-phase button's label
         is matched against a list that is necessarily English and necessarily a guess at wording,
         and a label that is not recognised is indistinguishable from no button at all. So the
         window's button labels are now logged verbatim whenever the set changes, tagged with the
         phase, and every give-up message names the buttons that were on screen. If Commence still
         does not fire, /xllog will contain the line naming what the button actually says.
         Compile-verified Debug and Release (0 warnings).

         Prior build - 1.0.26.0
         (Critical Engagement sign-up rewritten - it was a one-click guess at a two-click flow,
         which is why "sign up all" only ever reopened the window):
         Reported symptom: pressing "Sign up ALL for the engagement" spams the recruitment window
         open and never signs up or commences. It could not have worked. Four separate reasons.

         1. JOINING IS TWO CLICKS IN TWO PHASES, AND THE CODE FIRED ONCE. From the Patch 5.35
         notes, critical engagements "do not require you to be present in the field to
         participate. Instead, players must request deployment via the Resistance Recruitment
         window." That request is REGISTER during the ~60s registration phase, which enters a
         lottery against the 24/48 participant cap; then, only if the lottery picks you, COMMENCE
         during the ~120s preparation phase, which is the click that actually puts you in.
         "Permission to join critical engagement granted! N minute remains to deploy" is the
         message that opens phase two. It is one button whose LABEL changes - Register, then
         Withdraw once you are in the lottery, then Commence once selected - so a single press can
         never complete a join no matter what it sends. SignUpRunner is now a latched phase
         machine (Opening, Registering, AwaitingSelection, Commencing) with a per-phase budget:
         8s to open the window, 200s to wait out the draw, 20s to be deployed. The old TIMEOUT FOR
         THE WHOLE ATTEMPT was 10 SECONDS - shorter than the registration phase alone.

         2. THE PAYLOAD WAS A GUESS, SO IT IS GONE. The old code fired
         Callback.Fire(addon, true, index) - a bare int at a row index - with a comment conceding
         the shape was inferred rather than observed. Nothing anywhere drives this addon: no
         plugin in the local corpus of several hundred references it, and FFXIVClientStructs
         exposes no join method on AgentMycBattleAreaInfo (checked: its vtable is the plain agent
         one). Every windowed list addon in ECommons that has per-row buttons uses a leading
         command selector rather than a bare index, so the guess was very likely wrong - and a
         wrong payload is not inert, it is an unknown command, which is the most likely reason the
         window kept closing and being reopened. It now CLICKS THE BUTTON NODE instead:
         ClickAddonButton reads the AtkEvent the game already attached to that button and hands it
         back through ReceiveEvent, which needs no payload at all and is how ECommons drives every
         other confirm button in the game. The button's label is then also the honest answer to
         "am I registered", with nothing to guess at.

         3. THE WINDOW SPAM, WHICH IS THE REPORTED SYMPTOM. Show() was called on EVERY 350ms step
         where the agent was not yet active, with no latch and no cap, and the callback was
         re-fired on every step where a row was found - up to 28 of each per attempt. If the fire
         dismissed the window the two alternated: Show, fire, close, Show. Now: one Show, latched;
         one click per phase, latched; and the addon is resolved through agent->GetAddonId()
         rather than by the name "MYCBattleAreaInfo", which was only ever attested as a shipped
         ULD FILENAME - not the same thing as a runtime addon name.

         4. IT REPORTED SUCCESS FOR DOING NOTHING, IN THREE DIFFERENT WAYS. The verification
         watched the ENGAGEMENT's state, which advances on the game's own timer whether or not you
         did anything - so firing at a Register row and waiting for registration to lapse on its
         own read as "Signed up (Commence)". The timeout path said "Signed up, but the engagement
         state did not change", asserting success on a failure. And the YesNo handler clicked Yes
         on the FIRST Yes/No dialog found anywhere, before the agent was even fetched, without
         reading its text, and then reported "Signed up and confirmed" - so pressing the button
         with any unrelated prompt on screen answered it, on every box. Verification is now
         DynamicEventContainer's own CurrentEventId (added as CriticalEngagements.RegisteredEventId),
         which names the engagement this character is actually in; the blind YesNo click is gone.

         ALSO: TryFindJoinable returned the LAST Register entry rather than the first, and
         preferred a Commence-state row - which, with no prior registration, is a row you cannot
         join. Sign-up now refuses to start at all when the box is already in an engagement or is
         not in a field zone (it previously started with no preconditions whatsoever, from Gangos
         or a loading screen), is cancelled by Stop, and logs every step - the addon resolved, the
         button clicked and its label, the outcome. The multibox panel shows the phase and the
         window's real button labels, so "no button" / "wrong label" / "clicked and ignored" are
         separable at a glance instead of collapsing into one string.

         NEW DIAGNOSTIC - and the one thing that would settle the rest. Settings -> Zones now has
         "Log every UI callback to /xllog". With it on, press Register and Commence BY HAND on a
         live engagement: the log then shows the addon's real runtime name and the exact argument
         list the game sent, which is the whole of what nobody has ever written down. Send that
         and the click path can be replaced with the real callback. It hooks every addon in the
         client, so turn it off afterwards.

         A PREMISE THIS BUILD DOES NOT ACT ON, DELIBERATELY. If registration really is
         window-only, then walking into a Critical Engagement's circle enrols nobody, and the
         travel-and-hold path in Commit() plus CeSnapshot.IsJoinable are solving a problem that
         does not exist. Both comments now say so. The behaviour is UNCHANGED pending one live
         check - stand in a circle through the whole registration phase without touching the
         recruitment window and see whether you are pulled in - because guessing wrong in that
         direction would break the CE path that may currently be carrying the plugin.
         Compile-verified Debug and Release (0 warnings).

         Prior build - 1.0.25.0
         (The pathing overhaul - stuttering, wedging, walking into mobs, boxes scattering and a
         zone restriction that restricted nothing were five symptoms of nine defects):
         A full audit of the movement stack against the five failures reported from a live
         multibox run. Every item below was traced to source; the four marked ADVERSARIALLY
         CONFIRMED additionally survived an independent attempt to refute them.

         1. STALL DETECTION COULD NEVER FIRE, SO NOTHING EVER RECOVERED. [ADVERSARIALLY CONFIRMED]
         Two independent defects reset the same clock. (a) Movement.TravelTo's dodge-resume zeroed
         _leg to force one re-issue, but _leg is also what legChanged is measured against, and a
         Bozja world coordinate is hundreds of units from the origin - so legChanged read as
         "new intent" and ran the branch that resets _lastProgressMs, twenty lines below the block
         that had just carefully ADVANCED it past the pause, in the same call. That block's own
         comment says resetting it "is what used to pin RepathCount at zero forever, so genuinely
         wedged travel could never report itself as stuck". (b) Progress was measured as raw
         displacement since the last sample, which ANY motion satisfies - a knockback, a BossMod
         sidestep, or the plugin's own re-path loop taking two steps at an unreachable point - and
         the sample anchor only advanced when the test passed, so one twitch anywhere in the 8s
         window reset both the clock and the counter. Net effect: `stalled` never became true, the
         widening re-snap (the ONLY behavioural recovery in the codebase) never ran, `Stuck` never
         latched, and a wedged character reported "Travelling ... (18y)" with 0 repaths forever.
         Now: _forceReissue carries "re-issue" without carrying "new intent", and progress is net
         closure on the leg target (_bestRemaining), which running in circles does not produce.

         2. THE DODGE YIELD WAS A HAIR TRIGGER, AND THAT IS THE STUTTER. One true tick of
         AvoidanceIsSteering cost a full path teardown; resuming forced a fresh pathfind. With the
         400ms resume tail that is a ~600ms cycle against a 750ms repath interval - the character
         was being asked to restart a path more often than vnavmesh can deliver one, so it lurched
         and stopped instead of running. AvoidanceIsSteering is satisfied by any telegraphed zone
         existing at all, and IsNavigating is near-permanently true once Reborn's AI is on, so in
         an open field zone full of other players this fired more or less continuously. Now: two
         consecutive ticks are required before yielding; the 6s cap re-bases after a cooldown
         instead of latching avoidance off for the rest of the danger period; and a gap in the
         questioning (an engagement, an errand, a death) ends the yield episode rather than leaving
         a stale clock that trips the cap on the next route's first dodge. CombatApproach is fed
         the smoothed signal too, not the raw one.

         3. THE ARRIVAL LEASH HAD NO HYSTERESIS. [ADVERSARIALLY CONFIRMED] Entry and exit were the
         same expression, and the only one-way latch was armed behind `attackers == 0` - disarmed
         in exactly the situation the leash exists for. A stand-off attacker dragging the character
         across the boundary alternated RunDefend and travel, toggling the rotation and tearing
         down the approach path each way. The widened radius is now purely an EXIT threshold for a
         box that has genuinely arrived, and the latch is on distance alone.

         4. THE DEFEND BRANCH WAS CHOSEN BY AN UNLATCHED PER-TICK SCAN. [ADVERSARIALLY CONFIRMED]
         Its two branches disagree about the rotation, the approach and travel, so a mob whose
         target flickered swapped all of it several times a second - each flip a real IPC send in
         both directions plus a 700ms approach lockout. "Under attack" is now latched: immediate on
         entry, released only after 2.5s of quiet with combat genuinely over. Threat now also
         filters to hostile combatants; it counted any battle NPC targeting the player, which
         includes a companion chocobo following its owner, so a box with a chocobo out read as
         permanently under attack and would stop to fight nothing.

         5. THE ZONE RESTRICTION RESTRICTED NOTHING. PassesFarmFilter accepted any objective this
         box had not personally visited, and SkipUnknownRegions ships off with an empty learned
         table - so on a fresh profile every objective in the zone passed, and the only way to
         learn one was for the filter to have already passed it. The early return also sat ABOVE
         the Resolve call, so the positional estimate that three separate comments describe as the
         pre-learning fallback was never consulted on the one path it was written for. And
         ClassifyByPosition could not abstain (Anchors always holds all three entries for a field
         zone), so the caller's "region unknown" branch was unreachable in both zones and ticking
         SkipUnknownRegions silently became hard filtering on a three-point Voronoi over label
         anchors. Now: resolve first, abstain when the two nearest anchors are within 60y of each
         other, and only then apply the policy.

         6. A WRONG LEARNED REGION WAS PERMANENT AND SELF-LOCKING. LearnRegionHere recorded the
         CHARACTER's sub-area against the OBJECTIVE's id from an "arrived" the 30y leash may have
         granted, and RegionResolver.Learn overwrote on every disagreement - so a fight near a
         region boundary rewrote and SAVED the whole configuration several times a second,
         synchronously, on the framework thread, with several boxes of one install contending for
         it. A wrong value then filters that objective out forever, so it is never revisited to be
         corrected. Now: learning requires genuine proximity to the objective, and first sighting
         wins.

         7. MULTIBOX IDENTITY WAS COMPARED BY VALUE, INCLUDING POSITION. SharedObjective is a
         record struct and a skirmish ring drifts as the FATE progresses, so the same objective
         read as a NEW one five times a second - clearing the arrival barrier, the release latch
         and _committed on every tick, which made "everyone has arrived" unreachable and left
         _arrivedAtMs perpetually zero so the host's timeout never elapsed either. Added
         SameTarget (kind + id) and used it everywhere identity is meant. Also: the objective now
         carries its territory over the wire and a client rejects a foreign-zone one (the two field
         zones' coordinate ranges overlap, so it used to path to a real-looking wrong place rather
         than reporting that it had nothing to do); the host broadcasts "nothing" instead of
         latching its last objective forever; the objective and release latch are cleared on Start,
         Stop and zone change; a released client stops re-pinging ARRIVED, which could refill a
         barrier the host had just cleared for the NEXT objective and release the group early; and
         the Holding branch now releases the approach and drives the director instead of returning
         bare with a chase path still walking the box off the marker.

         8. THE DETOUR WAS GEOMETRICALLY SELF-REJECTING, WHICH IS WHY THEY RAN INTO MOBS. The
         waypoint was placed beside the ENEMY, so the leg to it ran diagonally toward the enemy
         before turning out; that segment's closest approach is a*C/sqrt(a^2+C^2), which with the
         shipped radii clears the 22y sight radius only past a=35y and never clears the 10y
         proximity ring inside 10.7y. The acceptance re-check samples exactly that segment, so the
         closer and more urgent the blocker, the more certainly the detour was thrown away - and a
         rejection was silent, had no fallback, and burned a full 1s throttle during which a
         mounted character closes 10-12y. It is now a lateral sidestep from the CURRENT position,
         so the first leg opens the gap immediately instead of aiming at the blocker. Also: the
         re-check no longer passes DangerIgnoreNearObjective (which excused every enemy within 25y
         of the exact spot we were about to stand on - a bubble wider than the sight radius it
         guards); the waypoint itself must be unoccupied, not merely the walk to it; a vertical
         tolerance rejects a sidestep snapped onto another Zadnor tier; the check interval is 250ms
         with a 100ms retry after a rejection; and the detour LEG is re-checked against live zones
         each pass instead of being committed to blind for up to a hundred yalms while mobs patrol
         and turn.

         9. SMALLER, ALL REACHABLE. A refused MoveCloseTo recorded itself as new intent, which
         permanently disabled the repath throttle and left an unconditional PROCESS-GLOBAL
         Path.Stop firing at 5Hz - tearing down CombatApproach's path and any other plugin's. It
         now counts as a failure, keeps its leg, guards the stop on ownership, and returns false so
         the caller's "vnavmesh could not start a path" diagnostic stops being dead code. The stall
         re-snap accepted a destination moved up to 80y and silently redefined "arrived" to mean
         it. DangerZone gained a vertical band, so a mob on the tier below is no longer a
         full-strength blocker. CombatApproach got a release/re-engage band and clears its lockout
         on release. The suppression map is pruned instead of growing until a zone change.
         DangerIgnoreNearObjective 25 -> 10, since it must not exceed the sight radius; the
         detour-clearance slider now floors at 2, because 0 silently made every detour unacceptable
         at every distance while every other setting still read as correct.

         WHAT THIS BUILD CANNOT SETTLE, AND HOW TO SETTLE IT. Whether StatusFlags.Hostile reads
         true for an IDLE, un-aggroed field mob is not answerable from source, and if it does not,
         that one filter empties the scan and the character walks the direct line through
         everything with the feature on and every setting looking right. The config window's enemy
         panel now prints the full scan census - combatants seen, and how many were dropped for
         each reason - and warns explicitly when every combatant was dropped for the hostile flag.
         Read it standing near idle mobs; if it says that, the filter is the remaining cause of
         "runs into enemies" and the line to delete is named in AggroAvoidance.
         Compile-verified Debug and Release (0 warnings).

         Prior build - 1.0.24.0
         (Party support casts at a named target instead of borrowing your selection - and the
         PrimaryCostType story this plugin has told itself since 1.0.20.0 turns out to be wrong):
         No new feature. This is 1.0.23.0's party support corrected, after the client itself was
         disassembled to settle how a duty-action press actually picks its target.

         THE COMMENT THAT WAS LOAD BEARING AND WRONG. DutyActions has said since 1.0.20.0 that an
         ActionManager.UseAction call "would have to carry the right extra parameter to be a
         duty-action press at all", because ClientStructs notes the game identifies a duty action by
         the Action sheet's PrimaryCostType (20 for slot 1, 21 for slot 2). That is a runtime
         LOOKUP, not a parameter: the client reads the row's PrimaryCostType, scans the loaded duty
         slots for the action id, and returns 20+slotIndex when it finds one (game function
         0x140903A30). Nothing passes it in. ExecuteDutyActionSlot is a wrapper that computes a slot
         pointer and calls RaptureHotbarModule.ExecuteSlot, whose Action branch is literally
         UseAction(Action, slot->CommandId, TargetSystem.GetTargetObjectId(), 0, None, 0) with
         extraParam hard-zero - the SAME call, differing only in the target you hand it. Corpus
         agrees: RotationSolver fires everything this way, and across BossMod, ICE, AutoDuty,
         MOAction, ReAction and Redirect not one caller passes a duty-specific extraParam.

         WHICH MATTERS BECAUSE AIMING BY SELECTION WAS WRONG TWICE.
         First, it can miss. GetTargetObjectId returns the SOFT target when one is set and only
         then the hard target. 1.0.23.0 set the hard target and verified the hard target - so a
         controller user, or any plugin holding a soft target, would have had the charge sent
         somewhere the guard had just declared safe. Silently. That is exactly the "applied to
         somebody I did not choose" failure the stop button was asked for.
         Second, the selection is not ours to borrow. This plugin's own CombatApproach decides what
         to close on by READING the hard target, and CombatDirector clears a hostile one on every
         travel tick. 1.0.23.0 already had to make the restore synchronous to stop the borrow
         tearing down the approach path on every cast; naming the target removes the borrow instead
         of timing it.
         So DutyActions.PressAt(slot, expectedActionId, targetId, targetName) fires the slot's
         action at one named object and never touches what is selected. The action is still the one
         loaded in the slot, so charges and recast are still the slot's - only the target is stated
         outright. GetActionStatus is asked first, because UseAction answers with a bare bool and
         ExecuteDutyActionSlot does not answer at all (it returns mov al,1 on both paths), so until
         now "Applied" counted presses rather than casts.
         DutyActions.Press - the hotbar route - is unchanged and still what the duty-action window
         clicks through. A person clicking a button should hit what they are pointing at.

         THE MULTIBOX PILE-ON, which was a charge multiplier hiding inside a virtue. The first
         sweep walked the party in party-list order, and the comment credited that determinism with
         making boxes "agree about who is next". It does exactly the opposite of what that implies:
         every box runs the same algorithm over the same party, so all of them pick the same first
         unbuffed member and eight boxes buy one buff with eight charges. The sweep now starts at
         THIS box's own position in the party list and wraps, which keeps each box deterministic and
         reproducible while systematically sending different boxes at different people.

         TWO LIVE BUGS AROUND ZONING, both of which the review found by reading the wiring rather
         than the driver. OnTerritoryChanged reset the holster driver, the mount and the aggro
         suppressions - and never touched party support, whose refusal table is keyed by object id
         and whose comment two lines above already says object ids do not survive a zone change. It
         now stops. And zoning WITHIN Bozja drops the director for a second, which the driver read
         as "not in a field operation" and terminated itself over; BetweenAreas is now tested first
         and pauses.
         Compile-verified Debug and Release (0 warnings).
         SEAM (in-game): whether the server accepts a Lost Action aimed at an object that was never
         the client's hard target. Strongly implied - MOAction and ReAction do exactly this for
         ordinary actions by rewriting UseAction's targetId, and the action packet carries its own
         target - but not measured for a Lost Action specifically. If casts stop landing entirely,
         that is the thing that failed, and the fallback is the old shape: set the hard target,
         press the slot, restore synchronously. The refusal now has a voice either way, because
         GetActionStatus is consulted before anything is spent and its status code is printed.
         Also unmeasured: whether firing by action id deducts the duty slot's charge identically to
         pressing the slot. Inferred from the calls being identical.

         Prior build - 1.0.23.0
         (Party support: buff whoever has nothing, top up whoever is nearly out, heal whoever is
         lowest - and a stop button that is always the second thing you see):
         Requested: "Duty Actions pane should be transparent, for buff/status effect skills it
         should apply first to those who don't have it in the party then those who are more than
         80% through it's total duration. i.e. Bravery lasts 10 minutes, if person has less than 2
         mins(20%) apply, there should be a stop option so it can be stopped when applying to non
         party targets. if no more actions available, stop task. Healing actions should
         automatically target lowest HP user in party first", and separately: "When creating the
         loadouts, you should be able to select from a dropdown of clients to select who to apply
         to."

         THE 80% RULE NEEDED A TOTAL, AND THE TOTAL WAS NOT WHERE IT SHOULD BE. To say a buff is
         80% gone you need to know how long it runs, and the Status sheet does not say: 37 raw
         columns, not one of them time-valued. Action's only time fields are Cast100ms and
         Recast100ms, both 25 for Lost Bravery AND Lost Protect even though those run 600s and
         1800s. PrimaryCostValue is the holster cost, and the LONGER of the two has cost 0. A scan
         of every integer column of every scannable sheet for a value keyed by status id found
         nothing.
         It is in ActionTransient.Description - the tooltip prose - as a literal line:
           Action 20713, Lost Bravery: "...by 5%.\nDuration: 600s"
           Action 20709, Lost Protect: "...physical damage taken by 10%.\nDuration: 30m"
         600s is ten minutes, which is the figure the request quoted for Bravery, so the source
         agrees with the player-visible truth rather than merely being self-consistent. That is
         parsed at load in LostActionDurations, which means a balance patch that changes a duration
         changes this with it - no table to go stale.
         Two hazards, both handled. The text is LOCALISED ("効果時間：600秒" on a JP client), so the
         sheet is fetched with ClientLanguage.English explicitly rather than the client's. And 13 of
         the 99 rows carry more than one Duration line - Lost Swift has three, 10s/60s/30s - where
         the first is the action's own effect and the rest belong to secondary statuses, so only the
         first is taken.
         WHERE THE DURATION IS ABSENT - every Essence, and every instant - the 80% pass is SKIPPED
         rather than guessed at, and the settings list marks those actions "(no duration in the
         data - never topped up)". The rejected alternative was learning the longest RemainingTime
         ever seen, and it is worth saying why: your first sighting of a buff is almost never at
         t=0, so the learned maximum comes in UNDER the real total, which makes every buff look
         fresher than it is and delays the top-up silently and unboundedly.

         WHO IT CAN AIM AT IS A STRUCTURAL ANSWER, NOT A CHECK. The request asked for a stop
         "so it can be stopped when applying to non party targets" - so the stronger half of that
         is making the case impossible. Every candidate comes from PartyView, which is built from
         IPartyList, which covers the 8-man party ONLY: the 48-player alliance is not in it and is
         not reachable from it, so there is no way to NAME an alliance member or a passer-by here
         and therefore no rule to forget. On top of that, membership is re-derived live in the
         instant before the press, because the list a decision was made from is a tick old.
         Two API facts made real guards necessary rather than decorative. Dalamud's
         Svc.Targets.Target setter calls the native SetHardTarget and DISCARDS its bool, so a
         refused target fails completely silently - hence the read-back that confirms the game
         accepted it before anything is pressed. And IPartyList reports Count 0 when solo rather
         than 1, so the local player is simply absent from their own party; PartyView falls back to
         them alone, and a solo box buffs itself.

         THE ORDER, WHICH IS THE REQUEST RESTATED. Buffs: first anyone with nothing at all, then -
         most-expired first - anyone under 20% of the total. The two are not the same need: someone
         with no buff is getting nothing, while someone at 15% of a ten-minute Bravery still has
         ninety good seconds.
         They are two sweeps over the WHOLE configured list, not two steps inside each action, and
         that nesting is the request read literally. Per-action nesting would finish Bravery
         entirely - topping a second player up from 15% - while somebody else still had no Protect
         at all, and "apply first to those who don't have it" plainly means an unmet need anywhere
         beats a top-up anywhere. Within the first sweep the order is the priority order the
         operator ticked; within the second it is most-expired-first, because unlike the first those
         candidates are not equivalent.
         Healing joins the FIRST sweep, keyed on HP instead of on a status: lowest current HP first,
         re-decided before every single cast. It has no second sweep - there is no topping someone
         up towards full. A floor (65% by default) is what stops "lowest" meaning "whoever is at
         99%": there is always a lowest, and without a floor "nothing left to do" could never be
         true.
         A load takes a tick or two to land, which is long enough for the reason someone was chosen
         to stop being true - so when the slot finally comes up, the same test that chose them is
         re-run against the same sweep, and a member who has since been buffed or healed by somebody
         else is dropped rather than having a charge spent on them out of momentum.

         AND IT STOPS ITSELF. "If no more actions available, stop task": when no configured action
         is loaded with a charge up NOR sitting in the holster, the task ends and says how many it
         got through. Everyone-being-covered is deliberately NOT a stop - that is the task doing
         its job, and buffs expire - so it idles there instead and says so.
         The stop is a button on the main window next to the status line, a per-box button in the
         multiboxer, and BoxVerb.PartySupport so the host can turn it off on every box at once
         without focusing four game windows.

         WHY IT PRESSES A SLOT RATHER THAN CALLING UseAction WITH A TARGET ID. The corpus idiom for
         aiming an action is ActionManager.UseAction(type, id, targetId, ...), which would skip the
         targeting dance entirely - but ClientStructs notes the game identifies a DUTY action by the
         Action sheet's PrimaryCostType (20 for slot 1, 21 for slot 2), so that call would have to
         carry the right extra parameter to be a duty-action use at all rather than an ordinary use
         of the same action id. The same reasoning that put DutyActions.Press on
         ExecuteDutyActionSlot in 1.0.20.0 applies here: set the target, press the game's own
         button, and every targeting rule behaves as it does for a manual click.
         The previous hard target is put back SYNCHRONOUSLY, inside the same call, and that ordering
         was got wrong first time. Handing it back a third of a second later left the target sitting
         on a party member across a controller tick or two - and CombatApproach decides what to
         close on by READING the hard target, so every single cast tore down the approach path and
         made a melee box re-pathfind to the boss it was already walking to. On a 1.5s gap that is
         roughly a quarter of a fight spent re-pathing. Returning the borrow before the call returns
         means nothing else ever observes it: the approach, the director's hostile-target clear and
         the operator's own click all run later in the tick and see the target as they left it.
         The bet that makes is that the client captures the target when UseAction is CALLED rather
         than when the queued action executes - which is how the action queue is understood to work,
         and which the seam below says how to unpick.

         SIX DEFECTS FOUND BY REVIEW AND FIXED HERE, all of them in the new driver:
           - Begin() on an already-running task cleared the pending state, so a second click on
             "Party support ON" landing mid-load stranded a charge already pulled from the holster
             into a slot nobody would press, and the next tick stopped the task reporting that the
             action had never been in the holster. Begin() is now a no-op while Active.
           - The deferred restore captured the PREVIOUS target on every Fire, so two Fires in one
             tick made the second capture the party member the first had left selected - the
             operator's real target lost for good. Dissolved by the synchronous restore.
           - The restore also overwrote a target the operator (or RSR) had set during the window.
             It now reverts only if the borrowed member is still the one selected.
           - A UseFromHolster the game refused did not spend the gap window, so the identical call
             was re-issued on every controller tick, ignoring the configured cast gap entirely.
           - A party member the game refuses to target - mid-raise, in a cutscene, flagged
             untargetable - was re-picked every window forever, starving everybody behind them in
             the sweep. A refusal is now REMEMBERED for ten seconds, not merely reported, so the
             sweep moves to somebody it can serve and comes back.
           - "Out of stock" was conflated with "not firable right now", which would have ended the
             task every time an action went on recast. (Caught before review; noted for the record.)

         ALSO IN THIS BUILD, from the second request: loadouts carry the box they are FOR. The
         editor has an "Apply to" dropdown listing This box, All boxes, and every connected peer by
         name, and the row's single button says where it goes rather than making you remember -
         "Apply to Kallen" beats "Apply" when four clients are up. A saved box that is offline stays
         in the list marked, and the button disables and says why, because a BoxCommand addressed
         to nobody is ignored by every box: the safe failure, but a completely silent one.

         And the duty-action pane is transparent - ImGuiWindowFlags.NoBackground plus BgAlpha 0,
         applied in PreDraw because the window system reads both on the way into ImGui.Begin and a
         value set inside Draw would land a frame late. The title bar deliberately stays: with the
         background gone it is the only thing left to drag the window by. There is a switch under
         Settings, Lost Actions.
         Compile-verified Debug and Release (0 warnings).
         SEAM (in-game): the targeting handshake, which is the part that cannot be checked offline.
         Set target, press, restore - all inside one call - assumes the client captures the target
         when the action is issued rather than when a queued action later executes. If casts land on
         whatever was targeted BEFORE the cast instead of on the chosen party member, that
         assumption is wrong and the restore needs deferring by a tick again - in which case the
         approach conflict above comes back and CombatApproach needs to hold its enemy across the
         borrow rather than re-reading the hard target. The read-back guard means an outright
         targeting refusal presents as "The game would not target X - passing over them for now"
         rather than as a misfire, and party-targeted actions redirect to self on an invalid target,
         so the worst case is a charge landing on the caster rather than on a stranger. Also unverified live: whether IPartyMember.Statuses stays populated for a member
         who is out of range - the struct read succeeds either way, so a zeroed list would read as
         "they have no buff" and burn a charge topping up someone who is fine.
         NOTED, NOT CHANGED: the header comment above says this project is verified against Dalamud
         15.0.2.3 / FFXIVClientStructs 7.51.0.8543, and the dev folder on this machine now actually
