         Prior build - 1.0.22.0
         (Loadouts get a searchable picker and an Essence slot, and nothing re-applies a buff you
         are already running):
         Requested: "You should be able to search in the search when in loadouts and should allow
         you to assign the essences in another dropdown besides duty actions and have the same
         type. this should check against your status effect to make sure you're not wasting it by
         applying the same one."

         THE SEARCH. A picker listed 33 or 36 entries in MYCTemporaryItem row order, which is
         release order - so finding Lost Font of Power meant scrolling past nineteen things. Every
         picker now opens with a filter box that already has keyboard focus, so it is click-then-
         type rather than click-then-aim, and Enter takes the first remaining match. Two or three
         characters is usually the whole interaction.

         THE ESSENCE SLOT. Loadouts carry a third field, and it is deliberately NOT a third duty
         slot, because an Essence is not a slot: it is an item the game consumes out of the holster
         to give you a half-hour buff, and exactly one can be running. The picker offers the 36
         Essence rows (plain, Deep and Pure) and the two duty pickers offer the 33 action-type
         rows; the lists are disjoint by construction, so neither can build a loadout that applies
         cleanly and then does nothing - which is what the duty pickers could do until 1.0.21.0
         filtered the item rows out of them.
         This does mean applying a loadout now SPENDS something, where before it only moved icons
         onto a bar. That is the point of the feature, but it is not a detail to leave implicit, so
         it is stated in yellow above the list and again under the Essence picker.

         AND THE STATUS CHECK, WHICH IS WHAT MAKES THAT SAFE. An Essence applied over itself is a
         farmed copy burned for nothing; on four boxes, on a habit, it is a farming session. So the
         Essence is used only when the buff it grants is NOT already on the player, and a refusal
         reports which Essence is up and for how long. Refusing counts as SUCCESS, not failure -
         the loadout asked for a state and the box is already in it, so "apply to all" goes green
         across a group where half of them were already buffed, rather than training the operator
         to press it again.
         The same guard went into the auto-use driver, where the waste was worse and older: that
         list runs on a timer, so an Essence ticked in it was consumed every cooldown window for
         the entire half hour it was already up, and Lost Protect was re-pressed every eight
         seconds. Now an entry whose status is already running is skipped and the window goes to
         the next thing in the priority order.

         WHERE THE STATUS MAPPING COMES FROM, since the sheet does not simply say. Action's
         StatusGainSelf column is EMPTY for 98 of the 99 Lost Actions - only Lost Stealth fills it
         in - so this had to be derived, and it is derived two ways with different confidence:
           1. THE ESSENCES ARE A TABLE, because nothing derives them: "Essence of the Aetherweaver"
              grants "Spirit of the Aetherweaver", different words, and the icons do not match
              either (action icons are 646xx, status icons 216xxx). What is true, and is what the
              table encodes, is that the families are contiguous and in the same order in both
              sheets: rows 41-55 and 56-70 both map onto statuses 2311-2325, rows 73-78 onto
              2434-2439. Note the middle one - there is no separate "Deep Spirit of the X" status
              anywhere in the sheet, so a Deep Essence and a plain one of the same name share a
              status row.
           2. EVERYTHING ELSE IS EXACT NAME EQUALITY, at runtime, which is better than it sounds:
              "Lost Protect" the action and "Lost Protect" the status are the same string in
              whatever language the client runs, because both sheets are localised together - so it
              holds in every locale in a way that stripping an English prefix would not. Required
              to be exactly one status of that name, and the action self-targetable and NOT
              hostile-targetable, which is what throws out the three matches that are wrong: Mimic
              (whose same-named status 1056 is a CHOCOBO RACING status - a pure collision, and the
              reason uniqueness alone is not a sufficient filter), and Lost Incense and Lost Rend
              Armor, whose statuses land on the enemy. That leaves 27 self-buffs mapped with no
              hand-written ids at all.
         An entry with no status is never refused. That is the direction to fail: a missing mapping
         wastes at worst one charge, while a wrong one would silently stop an action ever firing.

         THE ONE FALSE POSITIVE, STATED RATHER THAN HIDDEN. Because a Deep Essence shares its
         status row with the plain one, using Deep over plain of the same name is a real upgrade
         that this check will nonetheless refuse. That is the conservative way to be wrong - it
         declines to spend rather than spending twice - and the editor says so under any Essence
         it applies to.

         The loadout wire format grew a third field and decodes a two-field one as "leave the
         Essence alone", so a box still on 1.0.21.0 in a mixed group is not broken by an
         instruction arriving a field longer than it expects.
         Compile-verified Debug and Release (0 warnings).
         SEAM (in-game): the two halves of the status table that are asserted rather than matched.
         The Essence runs assume both sheets stay contiguous and in step; a patch inserting a row
         into either would shift a whole family, and the only guard is that a status id resolving
         to nothing drops that entry. The Reraise pair (Resistance Reraiser and Lost Reraise ->
         status 2355) is name-inferred, not matched. Both fail visibly rather than quietly: the
         driver's line in the main window and the loadout's refusal both name the status they think
         is up, so an Essence reporting the wrong buff name - or one that never applies because
         something it does not grant is permanently "already running" - points straight at the
         table. Also unconfirmed: whether the game distinguishes plain from Deep by status Param,
         which would let the upgrade case above stop being a false positive.

         Prior build - 1.0.21.0
         (AutoUseLostActions finally uses a Lost Action: load, wait for the slot, press - and only
         if you say so):
         1.0.20.0 recorded this as FOUND WHILE DOING THIS, NOT FIXED HERE. This is the fix, and the
         part that took the deciding is not the code.

         WHAT WAS ACTUALLY WRONG. HolsterDriver called PublicContentBozja.UseFromHolster(index, 0),
         read a true return as "the action fired", and stopped. It does not fire. ClientStructs
         documents that call as "use lost action from holster INTO specified duty action slot (slot
         is ignored for items, which are used directly)" - one function with two behaviours, and
         the contrast in that sentence is the whole content of it. For an ACTION-type holster entry
         it loads duty slot 1 and returns; nothing has been spent and nothing has been pressed. So
         with the switch on, the plugin reloaded slot 1 every LostActionCooldownMs, forever, and
         fired nothing - while also quietly stamping on whatever loadout was in that slot.

         WHICH ENTRIES ARE WHICH, READ OUT OF THE SHEET RATHER THAN ASSUMED. MYCTemporaryItem.Type
         is the discriminator, and LostActionCatalog had been dropping the column on the floor.
         Over the 99 defined rows it takes exactly two values:
           Type 1, ACTION - 33 rows. Lost Cure, Lost Manawall, Lost Font of Power, the Banners,
             Lost Excellence, Lost Chainspell, and so on. Loaded into a slot. Must be pressed.
           Type 2, ITEM - 66 rows, and this is much wider than the "potions and kits" the 1.0.20.0
             note guessed at: EVERY Essence, Deep Essence and Pure Essence, plus Dynamis Dice,
             Resistance Phoenix, Resistance Reraiser, the potion/ether/medi kits, Resistance
             Potion, Lodestone, Light Curtain and Resistance Elixir. Consumed outright by the
             holster call. No duty slot is involved, and pressing one afterwards would fire
             whatever else the slot happened to hold.
         Cross-checked against Category: every Type 2 row and no Type 1 row sits in
         MYCTemporaryItemUICategory row 7, "Item-related". Two independent columns agreeing is why
         this is stated as fact rather than as an inference.

         THE PRESS IS A SECOND TICK, NOT A SECOND LINE. UseFromHolster returns before the game has
         populated the slot, so pressing in the same tick reads the PREVIOUS occupant - and the
         failure mode there is not "nothing happens", it is a Pure Essence spent by accident
         instead of a Lost Cure. The driver therefore has a two-state machine: a load moves it to
         WaitingForLoad, and the press happens on a later tick only once DutyActions.Read reports
         the exact action id that was asked for. That id is handed to DutyActions.Press as well, so
         its own mismatch guard is a second lock on the same door. If the slot never comes up, the
         attempt is abandoned after 2500ms and reported rather than pressed hopefully.
         Two things fall out of doing it this way and both are worth having. An action already
         sitting in EITHER slot is pressed where it stands - no load, no churn, and a loadout the
         operator set by hand gets used rather than overwritten. And a press refused for charges
         lets the next action in the priority order have the window instead of wasting it.

         SPENDING IS ITS OWN OPT-IN, BECAUSE COMPLETING THIS CHANGES WHAT A TICKED BOX COSTS.
         AutoUseLostActions has never once spent an action-type charge. Anyone who turned it on did
         so knowing - or at least experiencing - that it cost them nothing beyond the item entries.
         Finishing the feature under the same switch would mean that on next load, without them
         touching anything, a farmed stock starts draining on an 8-second timer. So the press is
         Configuration.AutoFireLostActions, default off: a sub-checkbox under the existing one with
         the difference between the two kinds spelled out beside it in yellow. With it off,
         action-type entries are now SKIPPED rather than loaded - which is exactly what the old
         behaviour amounted to, minus the pointless slot churn - and the picker marks each ticked
         action "(not pressed)", so a switch that does nothing says so instead of being inferred
         from the absence of an effect. Item-type entries are untouched either way: they were
         genuinely working, and they are the one thing that switch has always really done.

         ALSO, FROM THE SAME COLUMN: the multiboxer's loadout slot picker no longer offers
         item-type entries. A duty slot cannot hold an Essence, so every one of those 66 rows was a
         loadout that would apply cleanly and then do nothing.

         And README.md's "Corrections to the source research document" is itself corrected. It
         claimed UseFromHolster "removes the need for any ActionManager duty-slot guesswork" - the
         doc comment read backwards, and the sentence this driver was built on. It now carries the
         Type table above and says what the press actually is. The 1.0.11.0 note further down this
         file that made the same claim is marked wrong in place.
         Compile-verified Debug and Release (0 warnings).
         SEAM (in-game): how long the game really takes to populate a duty slot after
         UseFromHolster. The 2500ms window is reasoned, not measured - it only had to be longer
         than a tick and shorter than a cooldown window. Watch the driver's own line: repeated
