         WHAT THE GAME WILL NOT CHECK FOR US. ClientStructs documents ExecuteDutyActionSlot as not
         validating that the slot is in an executable state and as returning true regardless, so
         its return value carries no information and every guard is this plugin's to make:
         framework thread, a living character, a slot that is actually loaded, and a charge up.
         Pressing an empty or recharging slot is otherwise completely silent, and a silent hotbar
         is indistinguishable from a broken one - so the refusal is printed under the bar and fades.

         A REMOTE PRESS NAMES THE ACTION, NOT JUST THE SLOT. A peer's row is drawn from a roster up
         to half a second old and any box can reload its slots from the holster at any moment, so
         "press slot 1" on its own could fire an Essence the operator never chose. The instruction
         carries the action id the operator was looking at, and the far end refuses a mismatch by
         name rather than resolving it by guessing. Lost Actions are farmed time.

         ONE CODE PATH, AND IT IS THE EXISTING ONE. A click cannot touch game memory - ImGui draws
         on a thread that may not - so a click only ever enqueues a BoxCommand, the same addressed
         one-shot instruction the multiboxer panel already sends. Your own slot goes through
         SendCommandLocal, a peer's through SendCommand, and the controller carries out both, so
         pressing your own slot and pressing someone else's cannot drift apart. The host/client
         rule is unchanged and now stated in the tooltip rather than only in the panel: your own
         two slots always answer, a peer's answers only from the host box.

         A SLOT IS A REAL BUTTON, NOT AN IMAGE WITH A CLICK TEST ON IT. The obvious way to make an
         icon pressable - ImGui.Image, then IsItemHovered plus IsMouseClicked - fires the action and
         starts DRAGGING THE WINDOW on the same press. Image and Dummy submit their item with id
         zero, which never claims g.HoveredId, and a click with ActiveId and HoveredId both zero is
         exactly what ImGui reads as a click on the window background. The bar would have walked
         across the screen over a session of pressing it. An InvisibleButton with the icon painted
         into it through the draw list takes the id, reserves the identical rect so the layout does
         not move, and presses on release so sliding off a slot cancels. The empty-slot placeholder
         stays an id-less Dummy on purpose - nothing to press there, and it leaves one place to grab
         the window by.

         AND THE INSTRUCTION QUEUE NOW DRAINS AT FRAME RATE. It used to sit at the top of the
         throttled 200ms controller tick, which was invisible for "go tap the cache" and is not for
         a hotbar press - a button that answers somewhere between instantly and a fifth of a second
         late reads as a button that sometimes does not work. Draining an empty queue every frame
         costs nothing.

         Click-to-use is on by default, with a switch under Settings, Lost Actions. A hotbar you
         cannot press is a display; a window kept open beside a rotation is also one you will
         eventually click by accident, and the switch puts it back to read-only without closing it.
         Compile-verified Debug and Release (0 warnings).
         SEAM (in-game): ExecuteDutyActionSlot appears in no plugin in the local corpus, so this is
         the first place that signature is exercised. If a press does nothing at all while the bar
         plainly shows a charge up, that call is the thing to look at - everything around it (which
