         WHERE THE DATA COMES FROM. The holster is the stock of Lost Actions you own; the two
         DUTY ACTION slots are what is loaded and pressable, and the game manages those through
         its generic DutyActionManager - ActionId[i], CurCharges[i], MaxCharges[i] and Recast[i]
         (the charge arrays are sized 2, which is where the "two slots" comes from). That is the
         same source the game's own duty hotbar renders from, which is what makes a peer's slot
         displayable on equal terms with your own rather than approximated.
         Note Recast is a RESOURCE GAUGE, not a countdown: Elapsed climbs toward Total, and a
         multi-charge action subtracts one charge's worth on use rather than zeroing it. So "time
         to the next charge" is the remainder of the current charge's slice, computed per charge -
         reading Total minus Elapsed would show the time to FULL charges instead, which for a
         three-charge Lost Action is wrong by up to two full recharges.
         ON THE WIRE. Rides the existing multibox pipe, no second transport: clients send their
         own loadout twice a second, the host folds its own row in and pushes the combined roster
         back out, so every box draws one agreed list. That is N sends each way per second rather
         than N-squared relaying. The host builds its own view from the very list it transmits,
         so host and clients cannot disagree about what the roster is. Encoding is flat text with
         cooldowns in tenths of a second, and character names are sanitised of the separator
         characters so a name can never corrupt the framing.
         SMOOTHNESS WITHOUT TRAFFIC. Your own row is read every frame - it is two pointer
         dereferences - so it is perfectly smooth for free. Peer rows arrive twice a second and
         have their cooldowns AGED FORWARD locally in between, crediting any charge that would
         have finished, so a remote sweep runs down smoothly instead of stepping once a second
         and a peer never reads "0 charges, 0s left" while waiting for an update.
         Rendering mirrors the game: icon from the Action sheet, bottom-up cooldown wash with the
         remaining seconds over it, charge count when the action has more than one, a green
         border and full brightness only when something is actually pressable - so "who still has
         an Essence up" is readable across the room without reading any numbers. Empty slots keep
         their space so rows stay aligned. Your own row is first and labelled.
         Works alone: with multibox off or the link down it still shows your own two slots rather
         than an empty window.
         Opened with /bbr duty (or /bbr actions), or the button on the Multibox tab.
         Guarded against the load-failure class this project has already paid for once: the
         cooldown tint is a plain colour converted inside Draw rather than an ImGui call in a
         static initialiser, which would otherwise run during the plugin constructor on a
         threadpool thread with no ImGui frame in scope.
         Compile-verified Debug and Release (0 warnings).
         SEAM (in-game): the per-charge cooldown arithmetic is read from the RecastDetail
