         Prior build - 1.0.13.0
         (The dodge gate was reading "the AI has a destination" as "the AI is dodging", so the
         runner yielded forever and BossMod walked the character off on its own errands):
         Reported: "It's trying to go to a quest but keeps saying yielding to bossmod, dodging a
         mechanic when there's nothing close."

         THE GATE MEANT SOMETHING ELSE. AvoidanceIsSteering was BossMod Reborn's
         AI.IsNavigating, which its IPCProvider defines as `ai.Controller.NaviTargetPos != null`.
         Reading AIController: NaviTargetPos is simply where the AI would like the character to
         be, set from its navigation decision for ANY reason - uptime, positionals, following,
         staying in range of a target, walking to an interact target. It is non-null essentially
         all the time once the AI is enabled at all, and it says nothing whatsoever about
         telegraphed danger. Keyed on that alone, the controller took the "dodging" branch on
         every tick: it suspended travel, issued no path, and printed "Yielding to BossMod -
         dodging a mechanic" indefinitely with nothing anywhere near the character.
         And the second half of the report follows from the first. Both forks only steer while
         vnavmesh's shared path flag is clear - so a runner that never issues a path leaves
         BossMod free to move the character continuously, and the original's own AI preset walks
         to whatever it finds, including a strongly-weighted goal zone around an interact target.
         That is the "trying to go to a quest": not our travel, but BossMod filling the vacuum our
         permanent yield created.
         The gate now requires Hints.ForbiddenZonesCount > 0 as well - the count of LIVE
         telegraphed zones, which is the half that actually means danger - so movement is handed
         over when BossMod both sees something to avoid and wants to move because of it.
         Otherwise travel keeps the path, which is the right precedence: BossMod defers to a
         running vnavmesh path anyway, so a non-dodge reposition losing to deliberate travel is
         exactly what we want.

         A HARD CAP, because a permanent yield is always a bug. Handing our movement to another
         plugin is done on trust, and no mechanic in this game justifies giving it up for more
         than a few seconds. Past 6s of continuous yielding the controller takes the path back
         regardless, logs a warning naming the situation, and carries on. Had this existed, the
         reported failure would have been a stutter and a log line instead of a dead run.

         FORK DETECTION NOW PROVES ITSELF. Detection keyed on the installed-plugin list, which
         reports what is installed and enabled - not what actually constructed and registered its
         IPC. This machine's Dalamud log shows exactly that gap: BossModReborn present with
         loadPlugin:true and no "Finished loading" ever, while the original BossMod loaded
         normally. Believing the name there would drive the Reborn surface against a fork that is
         not running, and every Reborn-only read would quietly return its failure default. Reborn
         is now only accepted when a Reborn-ONLY gate (Hints.ForbiddenZonesCount, AI.GetPreset,
         AI.SetPreset) actually answers; otherwise it falls back to the original.

         Dependencies now shows the two signals SEPARATELY - "danger zones active" and "BossMod
         wants to move" - with the resulting yield decision and how long it has been held,
         because conflating them is precisely what caused this. It also says in as many words
         that "wants to move with no danger" is repositioning rather than a dodge, and normal.
         Compile-verified Debug and Release (0 warnings).
         SEAM (in-game): the 6s yield cap and the requirement for a non-zero forbidden-zone count
         are both judgement calls - if dodges now get cut short on a long mechanic, the cap is the
         dial. With the ORIGINAL fork installed there is still no danger signal at all (it
         registers no Hints.* gates), so it never yields and never can; its avoidance comes purely
         from its own pathfinding while we are not moving the character.

         Prior build - 1.0.12.0
         (Two field failures: multibox peers only appearing after repeated toggling, and travel
         standing still forever):
         Reported: "The multiboxing isn't functioning well nor the pathing, I have to spam the
         multibox for it to eventually show them as a peer." Diagnosed against the live Dalamud
         log as well as source - the log showed this box timing out on the pipe every 6.000s for
         nine minutes straight at Debug level (so invisible), and ZERO vnavmesh path requests from
         us during a session spent standing in Bozja (territory 920). Both root causes below were
         found by reading real source and then adversarially re-verified.

         (A) MULTIBOX: THE ARRIVAL BARRIER WAS KEYED BY A NAME THAT IS ALWAYS "unknown".
         _arrived was a dictionary keyed by the character name each box announces in its HELLO.
         That name is computed in Plugin.SyncMultiboxLink, which first runs on the very first
         framework frame - at the title screen, where LocalPlayer is null and the name degrades to
         the literal string "unknown". Nothing ever corrected it: UpdateSelfName only assigned a
         field and sent nothing, and the comment claiming "the client's reconnect loop re-sends
         HELLO" was simply wrong, because the pipe has no keepalive (MsgPing was handled but never
         sent by anything in the repo) and so never drops on its own. So every client collapsed
         into ONE dictionary entry: PeerCount said 3, ArrivedCount said 1, arrived >= peers was
         never true, and every objective burned the full 45s barrier timeout with the whole group
         standing still. The same collision meant one client dropping erased everyone else's
         arrival record. And the user's workaround is exactly this bug's only cure - toggling the
         checkbox is the one code path that calls Stop() then Start(), forcing a fresh pipe
         connection and therefore a fresh HELLO carrying the real name.
         Fixed by keying on the connection id, which is unique by construction (it is already the
         key of _clients, which is why PeerCount was always right). Identity for a barrier belongs
         to the transport, not to self-reported display data. UpdateSelfName now also pushes a
         corrected HELLO down the live link, and the host treats repeat HELLOs as idempotent.

         (B) MULTIBOX: THE RETRY BACKOFF PUNISHED EXACTLY THE USER'S BEHAVIOUR. One 3000ms
         constant was used BOTH as the ConnectAsync timeout and as an unconditional sleep after
         every attempt. ConnectAsync is active discovery (it polls and returns the instant a host
         appears), but the sleep is dead time - so toggling multibox on the HOST disposed every
         client's pipe and dropped them all into a 3s blackout, meaning impatient re-toggling
         actively PREVENTED discovery, while toggling on a CLIENT cancelled the sleep and
         reconnected instantly. That asymmetry, with no UI feedback at all, is why it read as
         random. Now: a 1000ms connect timeout, and an escalating 0/250/500/1000/2000ms backoff
         reset on every success, so a host restart is picked up within a frame or two.

         (C) MULTIBOX: THE FAILURE WAS UNOBSERVABLE. Every connect failure was Svc.Log.Debug and
         the UI said a flat "Link down (acting alone)" whether it was still looking, had no host,
         or had lost one. MultiboxIsHost defaults to false on every box, so the zero-configuration
         state is "nobody is hosting" - unrecoverable and completely silent. The main window now
         distinguishes connecting / no-host / up, names the last error, and after ~8s of failures
         says outright that no host is listening and that exactly one box must be ticked as host.
         The first failure of each streak is logged at Information.

         Also fixed in the pipe while in there, each independently able to strand a box:
         a superseded client loop unwinding on a threadpool thread could clear _connected and
         _clientWriter belonging to a NEWER working connection (the link then read "down" with a
         live pipe and ARRIVED silently never sent) - loops now carry a generation and only the
         current one may clear shared state; the host wrote to StreamWriters from three different
         threads (controller tick, accept loop, ImGui "Start all") with no lock, and StreamWriter
         is not thread safe, so interleaved writes corrupted the line framing into the empty catch;
         a redundant OBJECTIVE (which the host sends to catch a reconnecting box up) cleared
         _released while BroadcastGo refused to re-send GO, permanently stranding that box for the
         life of the objective; ARRIVED was latched as sent even when the link was down; and dead
         MsgPing was removed rather than left implying a keepalive that does not exist.

         (D) PATHING, THE BIG ONE: TWO INCOMPATIBLE DEFINITIONS OF "ARRIVED", so the runner stood
         still indefinitely while reporting that it was travelling. vnavmesh comes to rest on a
         shell of radius `range` around the point IT was given - and we give it the SNAPPED point,
         because CE markers are routinely in mid-air or inside geometry. The controller meanwhile
         asked whether we were within `range` of the RAW marker. With d = the horizontal distance
         the snap moved the point, the character stops up to range + d from the raw marker, so any
         sideways snap at all can make the controller's test unsatisfiable - and it is
         systematically unsatisfiable in exactly the case snapping exists for, where the reachable
         point is on the near side of the geometry the marker sits in.
         The resulting loop was a silent deadlock: TravelTo's own early return measures against
         the snapped point, so it returned "arrived" and stopped issuing paths, while the
         controller saw "not arrived" and called it again every 200ms tick. Nothing escalated,
         because the repath counter, the stall clock and the widening re-snap all live BELOW that
         early return - RepathCount stayed 0, Stuck could never latch, and no warning was ever
         printed. Status read "Travelling to ... (18y)" forever. The idle staging path had the
         identical mismatch with a smaller range, making it easier to trip and permanent.
         Fixed by giving Movement a persistent arrival basis - the raw destination and the point
         vnavmesh is actually driven to - that survives Stop(), and making HasArrived measure
         against it. The two tests now agree by construction. Where the snap had to move the
         target further than the arrive range, the status says so explicitly rather than reporting
         a clean arrival, because the character may be standing outside the arena it aimed for.

         (E) PATHING: THE PLUGIN MODELLED vnavmesh AS SYNCHRONOUS, CANCELLABLE AND SINGLE-OWNER.
         It is none of the three, and every remaining pathing defect came from that.
         - Path.Stop clears the waypoint list but CANNOT cancel a pathfind that is still
           computing, and AsyncMoveRequest hands that result to the follower whenever it lands.
           So one stop is a request, not a guarantee - and Movement.Stop's old early return made
           the second and every later stop a no-op, exactly when a second was needed. Stops are
           now latched and re-pumped until vnavmesh reports idle, pumped every frame from
           Plugin.OnUpdate rather than the controller tick, because the stops that matter most
           (stopping the run, leaving the zone, unloading) are the ones where the controller is
           no longer ticking at all.
         - MoveCloseTo returns false when a pathfind is already pending, and both call sites threw
           that away AND committed their bookkeeping BEFORE calling. A refused request therefore
           recorded the new leg as live while the old pathfind walked the character to the
           destination we had just abandoned, with every recovery condition suppressed. The issue
           site is now the commit point, and travel refuses to enter the block at all while a
           pathfind is in flight - entering it would burn a Path.Stop that tears down the leg
           currently walking, for a request that gets discarded.
         - vnavmesh has ONE AsyncMoveRequest and ONE FollowPath for the whole process, so its
           "is a path running" gates carry no request identity. Travel and CombatApproach both
           asked "is a path running" while meaning "is MY path running", so a leftover travel path
           convinced the approach it was already closing - the character ran hundreds of yalms
           toward the old objective while the status said "closing on <mob>" and RSR fell back to
           its ranged filler, which is the exact failure CombatApproach exists to prevent. A
           NavClient owner is now recorded at the single point every request passes through.
         - A dodge used Stop(), which threw away the snap, zeroed the leg and reset the stall and
           repath clocks - so every mechanic silently defeated the repath throttle and pinned
           RepathCount at 0. Dodges now Suspend(): the path genuinely stops (both BossMod forks
           refuse to steer while vnav.PathIsRunning is set) but the destination survives and the
           clocks are advanced past the pause. Resume is held for 400ms after the dodge signal
           drops, because Reborn's AI.IsNavigating flaps at high frequency around a telegraph edge.

         (F) PATHING: THE COMMITTED LEASH WAS APPLIED SYMMETRICALLY, so it also satisfied the
         ENTRY test. A box that had once arrived and was then dragged clear - a chased fight, or
         far more often a death and the corpse run from base camp - stopped travelling the instant
         it came back within range + 30y and reported "At <objective>" while standing 40y+ outside
         the arena. The leash is now one-way, and only engages as a return trip when nothing is
         attacking (being pulled around by what you are fighting is what it exists to tolerate).
         Related: there was NO death handling anywhere in the plugin, so a corpse run carried a
         stale committed/arrived state across it. Added.

         The change set was then adversarially re-reviewed against the real vnavmesh and Dalamud
         source: 15 candidate regressions were raised and 14 refuted, the survivor being a
         diagnostic defect of this build's own making - the "searching for N seconds" clock was
         never reset on a successful connect, so a four-second reconnect after hours of healthy
         farming would have rendered as "Searching for 7200s" plus the no-host setup advice, i.e.
         it would have sent the user straight back to toggling the checkbox. Fixed before release.
         Compile-verified Debug and Release (0 warnings).
         SEAM (in-game): the real magnitude of the snap drift for Bozja and Zadnor CE markers
         cannot be measured offline, and it decides whether arriving at the snapped point still
         registers you for an engagement - watch for the new "Arrived as close as the navmesh
         allows - Ny from the marker centre" line and report any large N. The 400ms dodge-resume
         tail and the backoff schedule are judgement calls. Phase A-C cannot be reproduced with
         fewer than three boxes: host + one client passes the barrier cleanly either way.

