         AIConfig.ForbidMovement = false (NormalMovement stays on Pathfind - that IS the dodging),
         and Presets.ClearActive whenever a USER preset is in the active list.
         (2) THE REBORN GUARD 3 IS HARMFUL THERE. RotationModuleManager only adds the AI preset
         `if (_aiConfig.Enabled &amp;&amp; !Presets.Contains(ForceDisable))`, so Presets.SetForceDisabled
         - which for Reborn stops the global autorotation and leaves the AI untouched - would in the
         original remove the dodge engine along with everything else. It is deliberately not sent.
         Worse, the original force-disables ITSELF on death in combat and on a ninja pull, which
         would silently drop the dodging; the heartbeat now re-clears that whenever it appears.
         (3) THE ORIGINAL HAS NONE OF THE AI.*/HINTS.* GATES. Its IPCProvider registers only
         Presets.*, ObstacleMap.* and the Configuration console. So the original is driven through
         BossMod.Configuration ("AIConfig Enabled/ForbidActions/ForbidMovement") - which is READABLE
         as well as writable, so its heartbeat is read-before-write and touches nothing (no
         Modified, no config save) when nothing drifted - and its multi-preset active list is
         snapshotted with Presets.GetActiveList/SetActiveList (its GetActive only answers when
         exactly one preset is active). "/vbm ai on|off" and "/vbm cfg" are the chat fallbacks;
         the deprecated /vbmai alias is not used.
         (4) BOTH FORKS YIELD TO A RUNNING VNAVMESH PATH. MovementOverride.RMIWalkDetour in both
         computes movementAllowed with `&amp;&amp; !FollowPathActive()`, which reads vnavmesh's shared
         "vnav.PathIsRunning" flag. Two consequences. (a) There is no movement contention with the
         original despite it having no IsNavigating gate: it simply cannot move while we path, and
         moves freely when we do not. (b) A REAL DEFECT IN THE EXISTING REBORN PATH: RunTravel and
         RunIdle "yielded" to a dodge by returning without issuing a new path, but the path already
         running was left running - so vnavmesh kept walking the character through the mechanic
         while Reborn politely waited for the path to end. Both branches now call Movement.Stop();
         TravelTo re-snaps and re-issues from wherever the dodge finished.
         (5) THE ORIGINAL CLOSES ON THE TARGET ITSELF. FollowSlot.Execute has a fallback that adds
         a goal zone around the player's hostile target whenever no other module has one (3y for
         melee/tank, 25y otherwise) - the exact thing Reborn does NOT do solo, which is why
         CombatApproach exists. With the original, CombatApproach now stands down entirely
         (CombatDirector.AvoidanceOwnsApproach) - an approach path of ours would only make BossMod
         yield and lose the dodge. The flip side: FollowSlot does not check combat or death, so a
         target left over from the last fight would have the character walk to it during holds
         (CE registration wait, idle spot). CombatDirector.Travel clears a hostile hard target for
         the original only; RSR in Auto re-targets on Engage.
         (6) FOUND IN THE REBORN PATH: the ForbidActions read-back passed ["cfg","AIConfig",...]
         to BossMod.Configuration, but the console parser takes the NODE name first - "cfg" is
         the chat subcommand, not part of the argument. It has always returned "Config type not
         found", so ForbidActions was never restored on stop. Fixed; ForbidMovement is now
         snapshotted and restored too. The gate is now typed List&lt;string&gt; (exact match on
         Reborn; Dalamud JSON-converts it for the original's IReadOnlyList&lt;string&gt;).
         UI: Dependencies names the fork, explains the reduced telemetry for the original, warns
         when both are loaded; Settings > Combat describes both.
         Compile-verified Debug and Release (0 warnings).
         SEAM (in-game, original fork only): that AIConfig.Modified fired by the console gate is
         what pushes ForbidActions into the AI preset (AIWindow.ToggleTarget) - read from source, not
         observed; that Presets.SetActiveList resolves the saved names on restore (falls back to
         SetActive one at a time). Both forks: the yield fix means a dodge mid-route now costs a
         re-pathfind; if IsNavigating flickers on a long mechanic the repath throttle absorbs it.

         Prior build - 1.0.10.0
         (Load failure: the constructor read the object table off the framework thread):
         Reported: the plugin fails to load after a restart. From the Dalamud log -
         System.InvalidOperationException: Not on main thread! at ObjectTable.get_LocalPlayer(),
         thrown out of Plugin.SyncMultiboxLink at Plugin.cs:188, called from the constructor at
         Plugin.cs:100. The constructor throwing means the plugin never finishes loading at all.
         Dalamud builds plugin instances on a threadpool thread (ServiceContainer.CreateAsync), and
         API 15 refuses object-table access anywhere but the framework thread. SyncMultiboxLink
         reads LocalPlayer for the character name to announce over the pipe, so calling it from the
         constructor was always going to throw.
         NOT a regression from the last three builds - none of them touched this path. It stayed
         hidden because the method returns early when multibox is disabled, before reaching the
         read; enabling multibox is what makes the constructor reach line 188, and the next restart
         then fails the load. Anyone who never turned multibox on would never have seen it.
         The constructor call is simply removed: OnUpdate already calls SyncMultiboxLink every
         frame on the framework thread, so it only ever bought one frame. The name read is now also
         guarded on Framework.IsInFrameworkUpdateThread and degrades to "unknown" rather than
         throwing - the client's reconnect loop re-sends the real name anyway, and no announced
         name is worth taking the plugin down for.
         Checked the rest of construction for the same class of defect: nothing else built before
         the first tick touches the object table, ClientState or Condition.
         Compile-verified Debug and Release (0 warnings).
         SEAM (in-game): the multibox pipe now starts on the first framework tick instead of during
         construction. If a host and a client are launched at the same instant the HELLO exchange is
         one frame later than before, which the reconnect loop already handles.

         Prior build - 1.0.9.0
         (Three field defects: the pathing stall loop, combat plugins silently dropping out, and melee never closing to its target):
         Reported from the Southern Front: "running into walls and getting stuck", "not consistently
         flipping on RSR or BMR", "SAM is frequently too far from enemies and just spams a ranged
         attack." Three unrelated causes, all found by reading real source rather than guessing.

         (1) THE REPATH LOOP WAS EATING ITSELF. Movement re-issued a path whenever vnavmesh
         reported "not busy" - with no throttle and no memory. vnavmesh's AsyncMoveRequest reports
         idle for a tick or two between finishing a pathfind and starting to follow it, and
         permanently when the destination cannot be reached at all; its MoveTo also refuses
         outright while a pathfind is already pending. So the old code ran Path.Stop plus a fresh
         pathfind five times a second and the follower was torn down before it ever took a step -
         the character stands still twitching while the repath counter climbs, which is exactly
         "getting stuck". Retries of the same leg are now throttled to 750ms; a genuine leg change
         still goes out immediately.
         Worse, every one of those retries also reset the no-progress clock, so the stall branch
         (the one that re-snaps the destination) could never fire. Progress tracking is now
         separate from path issuing: real displacement clears the escalation, and repeated stalls
         re-snap with a widening query extent instead of retrying the same unreachable point.
         A stall while DETOURING previously did nothing at all - the re-snap was explicitly skipped
         in that case, so a wedged detour retried the same wedged point forever. It now drops the
         detour, suppresses that enemy for 30s so it is not immediately re-raised, and goes direct.
         Walking past a mob beats standing in a wall.
         And the destination was compared with exact Vector3 inequality against a live-read FATE
         ring / engagement marker, so a millimetre of jitter counted as a brand-new destination
         every tick - re-snapping, dropping the detour and restarting the stall clock each time.
         Compared by distance now.
         (2) RUNNING INTO WALLS SPECIFICALLY: THE DETOUR. The sidestep from 1.0.7.0 is computed in
         open space and offset ~28y perpendicular, which in the Southern Front lands inside a
         building or a cliff constantly. SnapToMesh always returns SOMETHING, so it was handing
         back the nearest unrelated mesh - routinely the far side of the wall the point landed in -
         and the run committed to it. A detour is now refused if snapping had to move it more than
         5y, and again if the new Query.Mesh.IsPointOnMesh gate says it is not standable reachable
         mesh. Refusing a detour just means travelling direct, which is the strictly better failure.
         (3) THE COMBAT PLUGINS WERE LATCHED, NOT DRIVEN. EdgeTrigger recorded what we ASKED for and
         never re-sent, but neither plugin can be asked what state it is in and both drop it
         unprompted: BossMod Reborn's AIManager.Update calls SwitchToIdle() the moment the party
         slot it follows stops being valid (a Bozja alliance does that constantly), and RSR's
         IPCProvider has no state getter at all, so its own auto-cancel or the user touching
         /rotation is invisible to us. Either way the latch said "already on" forever and the run
         continued with no rotation and no dodging. EdgeTrigger now re-asserts on an interval
         (Settings > Combat, default 5s, 0 disables), the re-assert is withheld while Reborn is
         mid-dodge because /bmrai on runs SwitchToFollow and would tear down the behaviour it is
         dodging with, and a fight starting forces one guaranteed resync.
         Found while in there: UseRotationSolver was dead config. The checkbox existed and was read
         nowhere, so unticking it did nothing and RSR was armed regardless. Now honoured.
         (4) SAM AT RANGE: BOSSMOD REBORN NEVER CLOSES, AND ONLY ITS SOURCE SAYS SO. AIBehaviour
         adds the follow-the-target goal zone inside `if (_followMaster)`, and _followMaster is
         `master != player`. Solo - which is how this runner works - the master IS the player, so
         that entire branch is dead. The fallback under it needs targeting.Target, which is only
         populated when an AI preset is loaded, and we deliberately clear the preset
         (AI.SetPreset("")) to keep Reborn from pressing buttons alongside RSR. Net result: Reborn
         dodges and does nothing else, the character stands wherever travel left it, and RSR - with
         a target it cannot reach - falls back to the ranged filler. That is Enpi on Samurai.
         New Automation/CombatApproach.cs closes the gap over vnavmesh like all our other movement:
         melee and tanks pulled to 2y inside the hitbox, everything else to 15y (job ids verified
         against ClassJob.csv), never flying (an airborne character cannot attack), and yielding
         completely whenever Reborn is steering. Approach and travel never hold vnavmesh at once.
         (5) AND THE OTHER HALF OF THE SAME SYMPTOM: the FATE arrive range was radius*0.5, which on
         a Bozja skirmish ring is 30y+ from the centre. "Arrived" fired that far out and armed the
         rotation against mobs well outside melee range. Capped at 20y.
         Closing on a mob then drags the character off the objective's centre, which would have
         read as "no longer arrived" and handed movement back to travel - walking away from the mob
         being hit and switching the rotation off on the way, forever. A committed objective now
         holds through a 30y leash.
         Compile-verified Debug and Release (0 warnings).
         SEAM (in-game): if RSR's "show toggled setting in chat" is on, each re-assert prints a
         line - turn that off in RSR or raise the interval. The 750ms repath throttle and the 5y
         detour snap tolerance are judgement calls; Dependencies now shows what was last sent to
         each plugin and how long ago, and travel status now names a genuine stuck state instead of
         hanging silently.

