         Prior build - 1.0.7.0
         (Enemy aggro avoidance: route around enemy sight cones while travelling, so the run stops walking into packs that can one-shot it):
         Requested: running fate to fate in Bozja drags the route through field mobs that can
         delete the character, so avoid them by field of view.
         Combat avoidance (BossMod Reborn) cannot help here - by the time anything is telegraphed
         you are already tagged - so this happens at the PATHING layer instead: do not enter the
         cone at all. New Automation/AggroAvoidance.cs models FFXIV aggro the way the game actually
         does it, as a forward SIGHT cone plus a smaller all-round PROXIMITY ring. That two-part
         model is the point: passing BEHIND an enemy is safe at a distance that would pull it
         head-on, so cone-aware routing gets through gaps a flat keep-out radius would refuse.
         Movement now samples the straight route for the first enemy footprint it would walk into
         and, if blocked, routes via a perpendicular detour placed on the opposite side to that
         enemy, offset by its outer radius plus a clearance margin. The detour is snapped to the
         navmesh and then RE-CHECKED - snapping can drag the point back into the cone it was meant
         to dodge, and committing to that would just walk into the enemy by a longer route. A
         detour is a single step, dropped on arrival and re-evaluated, which is what lets it chain
         around several enemies in sequence without any multi-waypoint planning.
         Skipped entirely while actually airborne, since ground enemies cannot reach a flying
         character and detouring would be wasted travel. Enemies within 25y of the destination are
         ignored - those are the objective's own mobs and routing around them means never arriving.
         Enemies already targeting us are also ignored; RunDefend owns those.
         Evaluation is throttled to 1s rather than run per tick: it walks the object table and
         samples the route, and re-deciding five times a second would also make the character waver
         between two routes.
         The level threshold defaults to 0 (avoid every hostile enemy, the safe default while
         travelling). Settings > Movement lists nearby hostiles with the levels the game ACTUALLY
         reports, so the threshold can be set from observation instead of from an assumption about
         what Bozja mobs read as - which is deliberately not hardcoded here.
         Compile-verified Debug and Release (0 warnings).
         SEAM (in-game): the default sight radius (22y), cone (120 deg) and proximity ring (10y) are
         reasonable general FFXIV values, not measured Bozja ones - all four are sliders. If the run
         still pulls things, widen them; if it detours constantly through open ground, narrow them
         or raise the level threshold using the nearby-enemy readout.

         Prior build - 1.0.6.0
         (Targeting: fight back when aggroed instead of running to the next skirmish, and stop abandoning the current objective mid-fight):
         Reported: "when another fate is up it prioritises running to the next fate instead of
         attacking aggroed mobs." Two independent causes, both fixed.
         (1) THE ROTATION IS OFF WHILE TRAVELLING. CombatDirector.Travel calls Rotation.Disengage
         by design (we do not want RSR pulling things en route), but nothing ever turned it back
         on for mobs that aggroed onto us, so the character ran across the zone being beaten on and
         never retaliated. RunTravel now checks for attackers FIRST and hands off to a new
         RunDefend: stop, dismount (you cannot fight mounted), arm the rotation, clear them, then
         resume travel on the next tick. Keyed on hostiles actually targeting the player (new
         Automation/Threat.cs) rather than ConditionFlag.InCombat, which lingers after the last mob
         dies and would make the runner stop for phantom fights.
         (2) THE OBJECTIVE WAS RE-RANKED EVERY TICK. ResolveObjective ran the selector from scratch
         each pass with no hysteresis, so a newly spawned skirmish that ranked higher - a lower id
         in deterministic/multibox mode, or simply a nearer one solo - immediately became the new
         target and the character walked out of the fight it was in. Added stickiness: a committed
         objective is kept while it is still worth doing (skirmish still running and incomplete;
         engagement still registering with enough margin to arrive), and only then is a fresh pick
         made. The multibox host re-broadcasts the sticky objective so followers do not diverge.
         Both are on by default and independently toggleable in Settings > Engagements.
         Compile-verified Debug and Release (0 warnings).
         SEAM (in-game): the 30y attacker radius is a judgement call - far enough to catch what is
         actually on you, close enough that a mob you have already outrun does not hold the run up.

